ue above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**Current Thinking: River Decision**\n\nOkay, here we go. Hand 11 of 20. Chips are looking good, but I need to make a solid decision here. I’ve got Ace-Ten of Clubs. The board is: Six of Hearts, Three of Hearts, Ace of Spades, Five of Clubs, and the King of Clubs. I’ve bet 200 on each street.\n\nMy hand is a pair of Aces with King, Ten, and Six kickers. That\'s a solid hand, but this board is tricky. I’m thinking about what my opponent could have, considering their calling behavior throughout the hand. They’ve been passive, just calling on every street. That suggests a weak-ish hand, potentially a marginal Ace or a small pair, or maybe a missed draw. A flush is out, I didn’t hit the clubs.\n\nThe king on the river gives me pause. It doesn’t change my hand but it could have given them two pair or a better kicker. If they have an Ace with a better kicker, I am toast. If they have a king with a decent kicker, that is also bad for me. It’s also possible they have a weak ace, a small pair, or just a missed draw.\n\nI have two options: Check or Bet. If I bet, I might get value from worse hands, but I risk getting called by better hands. My hand isn’t the nuts, so I don\'t want to over-value it. Given my opponent\'s passive play, checking feels right. A check will probably result in a check-back, and I win the pot if my hand is the best. If they bet, I can re-evaluate. I could be giving up some value by checking, but the risk of losing more chips to a better hand is too high, especially considering my lead in the match.\n\nThe "raise" option doesn\'t really feel right either. Betting a small amount might tempt them to call with a King or a weak Ace, but I\'m more likely to get crushed by a better hand. Given all that, I am deciding to play it safe and check, preserving my lead. Check it is.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: Currently, I hold a pair of Aces with a Ten kicker (A-A-K-T-6). The board is {6h, 3h, As, 5c, Kc}. The opponent has passively called bets on the flop and turn, suggesting a range of marginal made hands (like weak Aces) or draws. The River King is a scare card that completes A-K (Two Pair) and keeps A-3, A-5, A-6 ahead as Two Pair hands. Additionally, AQ and AJ beat my Ten kicker. I only beat weaker Aces (A2, A4, A7-A9) and missed draws. Betting here is unlikely to be called by worse hands (which will fold) but will be called by better hands. Checking allows me to realize my showdown value without risking more chips, preserving my tournament lead.\n\nReason: Betting has little value as worse hands fold and better hands call. The board texture (A, K, low cards) connects with many Two Pair combinations (A3, A5, A6, AK) that beat my top pair. Checking minimizes loss against better hands while winning the pot against missed draws or weaker pairs.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
