y lead.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nGiven the game state, my draw, and the opponent's behavior, checking is the clear play here. It minimizes risk and gives me a free look at the river to determine whether to continue or fold.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n0 (Check).\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation:\nI have Ace-Ten suited (Spades), which currently gives me Ace-High on a board of 2h-Qs-8h-Kd. I have a gutshot straight draw (needing a Jack to make a Broadway straight). The opponent has been passive throughout the hand, checking on the flop and turn.\n- **Checking (0):** This is a very strong option. It allows me to see the River card for free to try and hit my straight or a pair. Since I am leading the overall match and this is the final hand, keeping the pot small reduces variance and protects my lead.\n- **Betting:** Betting could take down the pot now, but it risks a check-raise or calling a bet when I am currently behind a pair. It adds unnecessary risk.\n\nReason:\nI currently hold Ace-High with a gutshot straight draw. Since I am leading the cumulative score on the final hand of the game, my primary strategic goal is risk minimization to secure the tournament victory. The opponent has shown no aggression, so checking back allows me to realize my equity (potentially hitting a Jack for the nuts or an Ace/Ten for a pair) without committing more chips. There is no need to bloat the pot with a drawing hand when a free card is available.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
